  treating or preventing complications experienced by people with SCD.", "background": "Globally, sickle cell disease   
  (SCD) is one of the commonest severe monogenic disorders, due to the inheritance of two abnormal haemoglobin (beta    
  globin) genes. SCD can cause severe pain, significant end‐organ damage, pulmonary complications, and premature        
  death. Red blood cell (RBC) transfusions are used to treat complications of SCD, e.g. acute chest syndrome (ACS)      
